8161163,"token":"."}],"message":{"content":"Echo State Networks (ESNs) are a type of recurrent neural network used for time-series prediction and processing. They consist of a large, fixed-size hidden layer called a reservoir, which is typically sparsely connected and randomly weighted. The weights of the reservoir are usually not trained; instead, the network learns only the readout weights that map the reservoir's state to the desired output. Training involves presenting input data to the reservoir and adjusting the readout weights to minimize prediction error. Reservoirs are trained by exposing them to data, allowing them to capture temporal dynamics without the need for backpropagation through time.","role":"assistant"},"model":"qwen2.5:7b","prompt_eval_count":1362,"prompt_eval_duration":44908000,"total_duration":1496698600}}